In V8, objects created with a null prototype (`{ __proto__: null }` or `ObjectCreate(null)`) start in dictionary mode: they are slow to allocate and property reads are hash lookups. See [Optimizing objects with null prototypes](https://adventures.nodeland.dev/archive/optimizing-objects-with-null-prototypes/).

The main problem this PR addresses is that internal code used `{ __proto__: null }` as a default parameter value in many places, which allocates a dictionary-mode object on **every call** that omits the argument.

## Changes

### Default parameters (main change)

Object literal default parameters are replaced so that omitting the argument no longer allocates anything:

- `undefined`, when the value is only passed to a WebIDL dictionary converter or is already checked for `undefined` (e.g. `TextDecoder`, `Blob`, `File`, `pipeTo`, `postMessage`).
- Otherwise, a shared frozen object created once at module load (`EMPTY_EVENT_INIT`, `EMPTY_OPTIONS`, and node's existing `kEmptyObject` from `ext:deno_node/internal/util.mjs`).
- Defaults that are mutated or exposed (`diagnostics_channel` trace contexts, `emitKeypressEvents`) default to `undefined` and allocate a fresh `{ __proto__: null }` in the function body only when omitted.

### `prefer-primordials` lint plugin

- Flag `{ __proto__: null }` default parameters in addition to `{}`, with a hint to use `undefined` or a shared frozen object.
- When checking that property descriptors passed to `ObjectDefineProperty`, `ReflectDefineProperty`, `ObjectDefineProperties` and `ObjectCreate` have a null prototype, also follow `const` bindings in the same file (and `ObjectFreeze(...)`), so `const desc = { value }; ObjectDefineProperty(o, k, desc);` is flagged too.

### Long-lived shared objects

`ObjectSetPrototypeOf({ ... }, null)` keeps a literal in fast mode, but it is **slower to allocate** than `{ __proto__: null }`. So it is only used for objects that are created once and then read repeatedly: the shared defaults above, and existing module-level constants read on hot paths (`kEmptyObject`, fs stat/readFile default options, `encodeIntoOpts`, webidl `EMPTY_OPTS`, ...).

### What is intentionally unchanged

`{ __proto__: null }` remains the right choice, and is kept, for:

- Objects allocated per call and read only a few times (property descriptors passed to `ObjectDefineProperty` and friends, per-call option bags, dictionaries returned by WebIDL converters), where the cheaper allocation wins.
- Lookup tables keyed by computed keys and dynamic caches, where dictionary mode is as fast or faster.

## Benchmarks

Release builds of `main` (b4f08f1276) and this branch, run alternately 3 times on an Apple Silicon Mac (median of avg):

| Benchmark | main | this PR | Speedup |
|---|---|---|---|
| `new Event(type)` | 34.4 ns | 11.1 ns | **3.10x** |
| `new CustomEvent(type)` | 36.0 ns | 10.8 ns | **3.34x** |
| `new ErrorEvent(type)` | 58.2 ns | 10.3 ns | **5.64x** |
| `new CloseEvent(type)` | 50.7 ns | 9.2 ns | **5.53x** |
| `new ProgressEvent(type)` | 92.1 ns | 16.8 ns | **5.47x** |
| `new TextDecoder()` | 84.2 ns | 49.9 ns | **1.69x** |

Heavier APIs (`Blob`, `File`, streams, `performance.mark`, `Deno.inspect`, `vm.Script`, node `fs.statSync`/`readFileSync`, `util.inspect`, ...) show no regression (1.00x–1.10x, within noise).

