`Layer::prepare` uploads the indices of every mesh in a batch, but `render` skipped a mesh whose clip bounds fall outside the layer without moving `index_offset` past its indices. Every mesh drawn after it in the same batch then read the wrong indices and drew the wrong triangles.

It shows when part of a canvas drawn with `Frame::with_clip` scrolls out of view while the rest is visible: the rest of the canvas is drawn with the clipped part's indices.

<img width="1570" height="640" alt="comparison" src="https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/29552b22-39b9-4915-807d-2ede496b94dd" />

Reproduction:

```rust
//! One canvas, twice as tall as the scrollable around it, scrolled to the bottom. Its top quarter is drawn with
//! `with_clip`, so it's a mesh with its own clip, fully out of view; the triangle pipeline skips it. The green circle
//! drawn after it, in the visible half, must still come out as a circle.
use iced::widget::canvas::{Frame, Geometry, Path, Program};
use iced::widget::{canvas, scrollable};
use iced::{Color, Element, Point, Rectangle, Renderer, Size, Theme, Widget, mouse};

struct Tall;

impl<M> Program<M> for Tall {
    type State = ();

    fn draw(&self, _: &(), renderer: &Renderer, _: &Theme, bounds: Rectangle, _: mouse::Cursor) -> Vec<Geometry> {
        let mut frame = Frame::new(renderer, bounds.size());
        // Many small red squares in the top quarter: many indices, in a different pattern from the circle's.
        frame.with_clip(Rectangle::new(Point::ORIGIN, Size::new(200.0, 50.0)), |frame| {
            let grid = Path::new(|p| {
                for i in 0..40 {
                    p.rectangle(Point::new((i % 10) as f32 * 20.0, (i / 10) as f32 * 12.0), Size::new(10.0, 8.0));
                }
