Under on-demand wrapping, a re-exported snapshot can observe a later mutation even though its forwarding module is imported at startup. This change preserves initialization order when that forwarding module retains local initialization code.

```js
// state.js
export let state = 0;
export function bump() { state++; }

// value.js
import { state } from './state.js';
export const SNAPSHOT = state;

// shared.js
export { SNAPSHOT } from './value.js';
export const LOCAL = 'local';

// main.js
import { LOCAL } from './shared.js';
import { bump } from './state.js';
export const local = LOCAL;
bump();
export const load = () => import('./lazy.js').then((m) => m.SNAPSHOT);

// lazy.js
export { SNAPSHOT } from './shared.js';
```

Enable `strictExecutionOrder` and `experimental.onDemandWrapping`, preserve entry exports with `preserveEntrySignatures: 'allow-extension'`, and place only `value.js` in a manual chunk group with `includeDependenciesRecursively: false`. With these settings, `await load()` returned `1`; it returns `0`.

Here, tree shaking removes the re-export statement, and eager `shared.js` emits no call to initialize `value.js`. The on-demand planner wraps modules whose initializers would call an already selected wrapper, repeating through forwarding chains. It resolves those calls with every eligible module wrapped, since wrapping determines which module owns a re-export's initialization.

Transparent forwarding modules, whose wrappers own no initialization calls, and mixed modules dropped by constant inlining have separate initialization gaps in both strict modes, tracked in #10294.

