## Context
This PR supersedes #155 with two improvements:
1. Includes a regression test case for the validation logic
2. Created from a dedicated fix branch (`fix/add-validation`) instead of `master`, following proper contribution workflow

Apologies for the earlier confusion with the branch source.

## Summary
Added detailed comments to the Frame::check function's bulk string (b'$') case in src/frame.rs to explain the RESP (Redis Serialization Protocol) format and validation logic.
Added validation for special cases, aligning with `Frame::parse`.

## Changes
### Source code
**File**: `src/frame.rs`
Added comments and validation:
```rust
               // Bulk strings in the RESP protocol.
               //
               // Format: $<length>\r\n<data>\r\n
               //
               // Special case: $-1\r\n represents a Null value.
               // Validates that the frame conforms to RESP protocol.
               if b'-' == peek_u8(src)? {
                    let line = get_line(src)?;
                    if line != b"-1" {
                        return Err("protocol error; invalid frame format".into());
                    }
                    Ok(())
                } else {
                    // Read the bulk string
                    let len: usize = get_decimal(src)?.try_into()?;

                    // skip that number of bytes + 2 (\r\n).
                    skip(src, len + 2)
                }
```

### Test case

**File**:`test/frame_validation.rs`
```rust
use mini_redis::frame::{Error, Frame};
use std::io::Cursor;

#[test]
fn check_rejects_invalid_negative_bulk_length() {
    // Valid null bulk string in RESP.
    let mut ok = Cursor::new(&b"$-1\r\n"[..]);
    assert!(Frame::check(&mut ok).is_ok());

    // Invalid negative bulk length.
    let mut bad = Cursor::new(&b"$-2\r\n"[..]);
    let err = Frame::check(&mut bad).unwrap_err();

    match err {
        Error::Other(e) => {
            assert_eq!(e.to_string(), "protocol error; invalid frame format");
        }
        Error::Incomplete => panic!("expected protocol error, got incomplete"),
    }
}
```
All tests pass locally

## Rationale

- The bulk string case previously had minimal documentation
- Clarifies the expected RESP protocol format for new contributors
- Documents the special Null value case (`$-1\r\n`)
- Improves code maintainability by making the protocol validation logic explicit

I should have implemented this allocator years ago, but I kept putting it off. Better late than never.

This allocator is really cute because there are only 2 unpredictable branches in both the allocate and deallocate path, everything else being either the definition of a predictable branch or a conditional move. And there are no loops, making the time complexity *O*(1). Even better, it has almost ideal fragmentation, almost as good as `FreeListAllocator`'s.

I went all the way making sure I benchmarked this as scientifically as possible: a pre-generated large synthetic workload, not just a micro-benchmark, to really stress the performance and fragmentation, and a pinned thread with maximum priority to take the OS's thread scheduler out of the equation. Each allocator runs the same synthetic workload, and the minimum, average and maximum times per iteration are gathered, as well as the maximum region memory that it used up, which is a measure of fragmentation. I chose to only use `Suballocator::allocate_buffer` because that's where performance really matters in the end, and to give `FreeListAllocator` a fighting chance. The results:

```
                             min time            avg time            max time          max memory
TlsfAllocator        14.600401ms/iter    16.045816ms/iter    23.504215ms/iter             5542MiB
FreeListAllocator   229.613494ms/iter   234.086887ms/iter   347.655521ms/iter             5389MiB
BuddyAllocator       35.166483ms/iter    36.145468ms/iter    75.032304ms/iter             7145MiB
```
The exact numbers vary based on the specific workload that was (randomly) generated, but the trends are always the same:
- `TlsfAllocator` outperforms the other allocators across the board;
- `TlsfAllocator` has only a 3% worse fragmentation than `FreeListAllocator`;
- `BuddyAllocator` has a 30% worse fragmentation than `FreeListAllocator`.

All of which is exactly as expected. What isn't expected is that `FreeListAllocator` is *that* much slower than the rest, which seems extremely fixable.

<details>
<summary>Program used to run the benchmark</summary>
Needs to be run with priviledge because the program 100%s CPU core 0 at all times, essentially having it all to itself.

```toml
[dependencies]
core_affinity = "0.8"
rand = "0.8"
thread-priority = "3.0"
```

```rs
use rand::Rng;
use std::{
    cmp,
    time::{Duration, Instant},
};
use vulkano::{
    memory::allocator::{
        suballocator::{Region, TlsfAllocator},
        AllocationHandle, AllocationType, BuddyAllocator, DeviceLayout, FreeListAllocator,
        Suballocation, Suballocator,
    },
    DeviceSize,
};

const ITERATIONS: usize = 10_000;
const OPERATIONS: usize = 500_000;
const REGION_SIZE: DeviceSize = 1 << 35;
const MIN_ALLOCATION_SIZE: DeviceSize = 16;
const MAX_ALLOCATION_SIZE: DeviceSize = 1024 * 1024;

enum Operation {
    Allocate { index: u32, size: DeviceSize },
    Deallocate { index: u32 },
}

#[rustfmt::skip]
fn main() {
    let core_ids = core_affinity::get_core_ids().unwrap();
    assert!(core_affinity::set_for_current(core_ids[0]));

    thread_priority::set_current_thread_priority(thread_priority::ThreadPriority::Max).unwrap();

    let workload = generate_workload();
    println!("                             min time            avg time            max time          max memory");
    let (min_time, avg_time, max_time, max_memory) = bench::<TlsfAllocator>(&workload);
    println!("TlsfAllocator    {min_time:>15?}/iter{avg_time:>15?}/iter{max_time:>15?}/iter    {max_memory:>16}");
    let (min_time, avg_time, max_time, max_memory) = bench::<FreeListAllocator>(&workload);
    println!("FreeListAllocator{min_time:>15?}/iter{avg_time:>15?}/iter{max_time:>15?}/iter    {max_memory:>16}");
    let (min_time, avg_time, max_time, max_memory) = bench::<BuddyAllocator>(&workload);
    println!("BuddyAllocator   {min_time:>15?}/iter{avg_time:>15?}/iter{max_time:>15?}/iter    {max_memory:>16}");
}

fn bench<S: Suballocator>(
    workload: &(Vec<Operation>, usize),
) -> (Duration, Duration, Duration, String) {
    let (operations, max_simultaneous_allocations) = workload;
    let dummy_suballocation = Suballocation {
        offset: 0,
        size: 0,
        allocation_type: AllocationType::Unknown,
        handle: AllocationHandle::null(),
    };
    let mut allocations = vec![dummy_suballocation; *max_simultaneous_allocations];
    let mut min_time = Duration::MAX;
    let mut avg_time = Duration::ZERO;
    let mut max_time = Duration::ZERO;
    let mut max_memory = 0;

    for _ in 0..ITERATIONS {
        let mut allocator = S::new(Region::new(0, REGION_SIZE).unwrap());
        let now = Instant::now();

        for operation in operations {
            match operation {
                Operation::Allocate { index, size } => {
                    let allocation = unsafe { allocations.get_unchecked_mut(*index as usize) };
                    let layout = unsafe { DeviceLayout::from_size_alignment_unchecked(*size, 1) };
                    *allocation = allocator.allocate_buffer(layout).unwrap();
                    max_memory = cmp::max(max_memory, allocation.offset + allocation.size);
                }
                Operation::Deallocate { index } => {
                    let allocation = *unsafe { allocations.get_unchecked(*index as usize) };
                    unsafe { allocator.deallocate(allocation) };
                }
            }
        }

        let elapsed = now.elapsed();
        min_time = cmp::min(min_time, elapsed);
        avg_time += elapsed;
        max_time = cmp::max(max_time, elapsed);
    }

    let avg_time = avg_time / ITERATIONS as u32;
    let max_memory = format!("{}MiB", max_memory / 1024 / 1024);

    (min_time, avg_time, max_time, max_memory)
}

fn generate_workload() -> (Vec<Operation>, usize) {
    let mut allocated = Vec::new();
    let mut allocated_indices = Vec::new();
    let mut rng = rand::thread_rng();
    let mut operations = Vec::with_capacity(OPERATIONS);
    let mut operation_count = 0;

    'outer: loop {
        let count_upper_bound_magnitude = rng.gen_range(0usize..=8);
        let count_upper_bound = 1usize << count_upper_bound_magnitude;
        let count = rng.gen_range(1..=count_upper_bound);
        let allocate = rng.r#gen::<bool>();

        for _ in 0..count {
            if allocate {
                let index = if let Some(index) = allocated.iter().position(|b| !b) {
                    index
                } else {
                    let index = allocated.len();
                    allocated.push(false);

                    index
                }
                .try_into()
                .unwrap();
                allocated[index as usize] = true;
                allocated_indices.push(index);
                let size = rng.gen_range(MIN_ALLOCATION_SIZE..=MAX_ALLOCATION_SIZE);
                operations.push(Operation::Allocate { index, size });
            } else {
                if allocated_indices.is_empty() {
                    break;
                }

                let indices_index = rng.gen_range(0..allocated_indices.len());
                let index = allocated_indices.remove(indices_index);
                allocated[index as usize] = false;
                operations.push(Operation::Deallocate { index });
            }

            operation_count += 1;

            if operation_count == OPERATIONS {
                break 'outer;
            }
        }
    }

    (operations, allocated.len())
}
```

</details>

